AnimalTA's head and tail are the two ends of the animal's skeleton, kept
apart from frame to frame by distance. AnimalTA does not work out which end
is the head, so check that \code{head} is the head before relying on it.

Positions in the coordinates files are in pixels. Positions in a detailed
file are in the unit of the scale set in AnimalTA, and in pixels when none
was set. The file does not record which, so \code{unit_space} keeps its default
of \code{"px"}; if a scale was set, declare its unit with
\code{\link[anicore:set_metadata]{anicore::set_metadata()}}, and give \code{video_height} in that unit too.

AnimalTA writes the time since the start of the video in seconds in its
\code{Time} column, rounded to 0.01 s in the coordinates files and to 0.001 s in
a detailed file. The reader keeps it as \code{time}, so \code{unit_time} is \code{"s"}. A
detailed file exported without the \code{Time} column but with \code{Frame} is
timed by its frame number instead, with \code{unit_time} \code{"frame"}.

AnimalTA does not write the frame rate, but each row is a frame at the
rate it tracked at: the coordinates files give its number in \code{Frame}, and
a detailed file has a row for every frame. When every row agrees on one
rate, to within the rounding of \code{Time}, it becomes \code{sampling_rate}: the
frames between the first and last row over the seconds between them, or
the whole number nearest to it when that fits every row as well. Otherwise
it is left \code{NA}. In a short file the rounding leaves the rate uncertain by
about one rounding step over the file's duration.

AnimalTA numbers individuals from 0 within each arena, so an individual is
identified by its arena and its name together. The arena is its own
identity column, \code{arena}, holding AnimalTA's arena number (\code{"0"}, \code{"1"},
...), and \code{individual} holds AnimalTA's name for the target: \verb{Ind<i>}, or
the name it was given in AnimalTA, with its case kept. Every layout gives
the same values: the fixed layout's columns are named \verb{X_Arena<a>_Ind<i>},
the variable layout's \code{Arena} and \code{Ind} columns hold the same arena and
target, and so does a detailed file's name, \verb{Arena_<a>Ind<i>.csv}. A
detailed file whose name is not in that form is named after the file, with
its arena \code{NA}. \code{arena}, \code{individual} and \code{keypoint} are the aniframe's
identity keys, so functions that work per individual keep arenas apart.
The \code{arena} column is there for every file, also when it has a single
arena.
